{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetFromVertices","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetFromVertices","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OrthoschemeTiling"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.vertices_on_unit_hemisphere","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.vertices_on_unit_hemisphere","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L128-L136","doc":"**The three finite vertices lie on the unit hemisphere's boundary circle** — the\njustification that `hemisphere 1` is `geodesicPlaneThrough P₁ P₂ P₃` for the\nnormalized position (the vertices' circumcircle IS the unit circle).  Together with\nthe cited totally-geodesic classification (File A1), this is why the hemisphere face\nof the tetrahedron is the unit hemisphere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.mem_idealTetrahedron","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.mem_idealTetrahedron","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L118-L126","doc":"Membership in the ideal tetrahedron unfolded to its four half-space conditions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.idealTetrahedron","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.idealTetrahedron","kind":"def","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L98-L116","doc":"**The Milnor-normalized ideal tetrahedron with far vertex `∞` and three finite\nvertices `P₁, P₂, P₃` on the unit circle**, as the intersection of its four geodesic\nhalf-spaces, each oriented toward the interior.\n\nThree vertical faces (each through `∞` and two finite vertices, oriented toward the\nopposite vertex) and one hemisphere face (the unit hemisphere through the three finite\nvertices, oriented toward `∞`).  This is an *independent* object: it is defined from\nthe four ideal vertices via File-A1 geodesic half-spaces, with **no** reference to\n`chordHalfplane` or `inscribedTriangle`.  File A3 proves it equals the chimney.\n\nThe three finite vertices are taken on the unit circle (Milnor normalization), so the\nhemisphere face is `hemisphere 1`; see `vertices_on_unit_hemisphere`.  For finite\nvertices off the unit circle the hemisphere face would be their circumscribed\nhemisphere — the deferred general case flagged in File A1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hemisphere_disjoint_hemisphereExterior","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hemisphere_disjoint_hemisphereExterior","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L80-L94","doc":"The hemisphere face and its `∞`-side exterior are disjoint: on the hemisphere the\ncap height `√(R²−x²−y²)` EQUALS `z`, so the strict inequality of the exterior fails.\n(A sanity link between the geodesic plane `hemisphere R` and the oriented half-space\n`hemisphereExterior R` cut from it.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hemisphereExterior","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hemisphereExterior","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L71-L78","doc":"**The exterior of the hemisphere of radius `R`** — its `∞`-side.  A point is in\nit iff it sits strictly above the spherical cap `z = √(R² − x² − y²)`; because the far\nvertex `∞` is reached by going upward, this is the side of the geodesic plane\n`hemisphere R` that contains `∞`.  It is definitionally the floor condition of the\nchimney (`IdealTetrahedronRegion`), which is what makes the File-A3 identification land\non the nose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.sarea_circlePoint_unit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.sarea_circlePoint_unit","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetFromVertices.lean#L45-L67","doc":"**The signed area of a unit-circle chord against a point is `2 sin ψ (cos ψ − w·nθ)`.**\n\nFor two points of the unit circle at central angles `u` and `u + 2ψ`, the chord's\nperpendicular-bisector direction is `u + ψ` and it subtends half-angle `ψ`.  Testing\nany `w` against the chord (its signed area) is `2 sin ψ` times `cos ψ − w·(cos(u+ψ),\nsin(u+ψ))` — i.e. `2 sin ψ` times exactly the functional the chord half-plane\n`{w · (cos(u+ψ), sin(u+ψ)) < cos ψ}` compares to `0`.  This is the bridge from the\nvertex-defined `sarea` half-spaces (File A1) to the chord half-planes\n(`OrthoschemeTiling`): it lets File A3 derive the chords from the faces. ","signatureChunk":0}]}